        <h2>Seven checks that matter more than marketing copy</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="rd-buying-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Toy, pet, project or companion?</h3><p>Bittle can delight a robotics hobbyist and disappoint somebody who wanted affection. The category name hides completely different jobs.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>What survives without an app?</h3><p>Ask what still works if an app, account or cloud service changes. This matters far more for an AI pet than a remote-control toy.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Camera and microphone privacy</h3><p>Recognition and remote-monitoring features are useful, but a connected camera in the home is also a privacy decision. Read the current app permissions and privacy policy before buying.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Battery and charging behaviour</h3><p>A self-docking home companion behaves differently from a toy that must be plugged in after play. Runtime only matters in context.</p></article>
        <article><span>05</span><h3>Exact kit, not just model name</h3><p>MechDog, Bittle and PuppyPi configurations can change servos, compute, cameras, sensors and price. Compare the box contents line by line.</p></article>
        <article><span>06</span><h3>Support and replacement parts</h3><p>Servos, batteries and chargers are wear items. A serious robotics purchase needs documentation and parts availability, not just impressive launch videos.</p></article>
        <article><span>07</span><h3>Do not buy specs nobody will use</h3><p>ROS, LiDAR and computer vision are valuable only when the recipient genuinely wants to experiment with them.</p></article>
